---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Key Rotation">
  <h1>Key Rotation</h1>
  <p>
    Rotating the JWT signing key without breaking in-flight access tokens
    is a four-step process. The whole point of the
    <code>kid</code> claim and JWKS is that you can introduce a new key
    while the old one still verifies. With the file signer there is no
    "active" flag to flip: you append a new key with a future
    <code>not_before</code>, and it becomes the signer on its own once that
    timestamp passes.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Production runs the file signer (<code>GATEWAY_JWT_SIGNER=file</code>,
      the default) with a real keys file at
      <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code> — not the auto-generated dev key.
      See <a href="/identity/docs/installation/jwt-keys">JWT Keys</a> for the
      file format.
    </li>
    <li>You can edit that keys file and signal the process to reload it
      (the file signer re-reads the file on <code>SIGHUP</code>).</li>
    <li>Downstream services cache JWKS with a known refresh interval (e.g. 12 hours).</li>
    <li>You know <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> for the deployment.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Throughout this page, the keys file is the JSON document
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code> points at. Each entry has a
    <code>kid</code>, a PEM-encoded RSA private key, and optional
    <code>not_before</code> / <code>expires_at</code> RFC 3339 timestamps.
    The active signing key is whichever in-force entry has the latest
    <code>not_before</code> (<code>not_before &lt;= now &lt; expires_at</code>);
    every key in the file is published to JWKS regardless.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Generate a new key</h2>
  <Code code={`openssl genrsa -out k-2026-08.pem 2048`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Step 2 — Add the new key with a future not_before</h2>
  <p>
    Add the new key to the keys file alongside the current one, giving it a
    <code>not_before</code> in the future. After the process reloads the
    file (send it <code>SIGHUP</code>), the new key appears in JWKS
    immediately but is <em>not</em> yet used to sign anything — the current
    key still has the latest in-force <code>not_before</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {
      "kid": "k-2026-05",
      "not_before": "2026-05-01T00:00:00Z",
      "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...current...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n"
    },
    {
      "kid": "k-2026-08",
      "not_before": "2026-08-01T00:00:00Z",
      "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...new...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n"
    }
  ]
}`} lang="json" title="keys.json — new key staged" />
  <p>
    Reload the signer so it picks up the edited file:
  </p>
  <Code code={`# The file signer re-reads GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE on SIGHUP.
kill -HUP "$(pgrep -f cmd/identity)"`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Verify the new key is published. Both <code>kid</code>s appear, but the
    signer is still stamping tokens with <code>k-2026-05</code> until
    <code>k-2026-08</code>'s <code>not_before</code> passes:
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s https://auth.acme.com/.well-known/jwks.json | jq '.keys[].kid'
# "k-2026-05"
# "k-2026-08"`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Step 3 — Wait for downstream caches</h2>
  <p>
    Downstream services need to pick up the new key from JWKS before any
    token signed by it reaches them. Make sure <code>not_before</code> is
    far enough out that every cache has refreshed by the time it passes —
    for the recommended 12-hour interval, leave at least 24 hours of margin.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4 — The new key promotes itself</h2>
  <p>
    There is no flag to flip. Once <code>k-2026-08</code>'s
    <code>not_before</code> has passed, it has the latest in-force
    <code>not_before</code>, so it automatically becomes the active signer
    the next time the file is loaded. To make the switch take effect at a
    precise moment, send another <code>SIGHUP</code> just after
    <code>not_before</code>; otherwise it takes over on the next reload or
    restart. Tokens already in flight, signed by <code>k-2026-05</code>,
    continue to verify because that key is still in JWKS.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s https://auth.acme.com/.well-known/jwks.json | jq '.keys[].kid'
# "k-2026-05"   <- still published, still verifies in-flight tokens
# "k-2026-08"   <- now the active signer`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Step 5 — Retire the old key</h2>
  <p>
    Wait <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default 15 min) plus a
    safety margin after the switch. After that point, no extant access token
    was signed by <code>k-2026-05</code>, so set an <code>expires_at</code>
    on it (or remove the entry entirely) and reload. Setting
    <code>expires_at</code> in the past stops it ever signing again while it
    is still in the file; removing the entry also drops it from JWKS.
  </p>
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {
      "kid": "k-2026-05",
      "not_before": "2026-05-01T00:00:00Z",
      "expires_at": "2026-08-01T00:00:00Z",
      "private_key_pem": "..."
    },
    {
      "kid": "k-2026-08",
      "not_before": "2026-08-01T00:00:00Z",
      "private_key_pem": "..."
    }
  ]
}`} lang="json" title="keys.json — old key retired" />
  <p>
    Once you no longer need <code>k-2026-05</code> in JWKS for any in-flight
    token, drop its entry from the file entirely and reload again so it
    disappears from <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>JWKS propagation</h2>
  <p>
    The keys file is the single source of truth: every entry is published at
    <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code> (including keys past their
    <code>expires_at</code>, until you remove them from the file). Reloads
    are atomic — in-flight signing keeps using the previous snapshot, and
    only operations after the reload completes see the new set. There is no
    inline-JSON env var; all changes go through the file plus a reload.
  </p>

  <h2>Emergency rotation</h2>
  <p>
    If a key is suspected compromised:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Remove its entry from the keys file entirely and reload (don't bother
      with the staged <code>not_before</code> step — every token signed by
      it should be rejected immediately, and dropping it from the file also
      drops it from JWKS). Make sure another in-force key remains, or the
      reload is rejected for having no active signing key.
    </li>
    <li>
      Bring <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> very low (e.g. 60)
      temporarily so existing access tokens expire fast.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run <code>SignOutEverywhere</code> on every user (no admin RPC for
      this; do it directly against the datastore — e.g. a SQL
      <code>DELETE</code> over the refresh-token table — to delete every
      refresh token).
    </li>
    <li>
      Restore the normal JWT expiry once the kill is confirmed.
    </li>
  </ol>
</DocsLayout>
</content>
</invoke>
